Finally, I ascended the last of the six new roles in Slash'em, an ice 
mage. Since it was a drow, the only one of the five new races I have not 
yet ascended, is the lycanthrope. That will be difficult, so I will take 
my time.

Ice mages are supposed to be slightly more difficult than fire mages, 
because their initial attack wand is a wand of cold instead of a wand of 
fire, so you don't have the additional defence benefits of a wand of 
fire. Indeed, I needed six attempts to ascend an ice mage whereas I 
finished the flame mage in five attempts. What may be more significant, 
three of my flame mages reached an ascendable state, two of them dying a 
stupid instadeath, but only one of the six ice mages reached a stage 
where one could be reasonably sure he would ascend, barring stupid errors.

Remarkably, four of my ice mages died deaths that were firsts for me.

My starting attempt, Icewind, had a ring of teleportation in his initial 
inventory and put it to good use. There was a big shop on Dlvl 2 and a 
vault, which he entered via the "ad aerarium" door. After getting the 
money, he put on the ring of teleportation and got out again. Things 
seemed to start nicely. The shop had a bag of holding, a magic whistle, 
a key, a blindfold and a can of grease which Icewind all acquired, 
either by paying or having them stolen by Howly, his winter wolf pup. 
Leaving the shop after much shoplifting, Icewind was still level 1 and 
had only 12 HP; he saw a yellow mold ahead, which he avoided. His pup 
seemed to attack it. The big mistake was to whistle Howly back. "Howly 
bites." Of course, I thought that Howly was confused from hunger due to 
our extended shoplifting tour, so I threw him a food ration, which he 
did not eat. "Howly bites." Now Icewind was down to 5 HP. There was no 
way to get away from Howly in a single move, because he was standing in 
a corner of the hallway. My character had of course a nice attack wand 
(cold) and the freeze sphere spell, but what good is that against a cold 
resistance creature? The third bite of Howly killed him. This is the 
first time, I was killed by my pet. I first misinterpreted this as 
winter wolves not eating food rations. But in a later game another Howly 
even ate a food ration in a shop... So I guess he must have been 
confused or stunned from the yellow mold.

The next character was Icering. I alternatingly played humans and drows, 
so he was a drow. His goody item was a +5 ring of gain constitution, 
which he put on to gain hit points fast. There was an altar to Air 
(lawful) on Dlvl 1, and a crossbow named Hellfire lying around. Picked 
it up without thinking, but being chaotic, nothing happened. Without 
crossbow bolts it is useless, and I never got to get them. The death was 
unspectacular, he was killed by a baby deep dragon while running away 
from a nymph. He could not put it to sleep fast enough and so lost his 
48 HP...

Icerider, human again, remains in my memory mostly for his death, from 
which I learned a new thing. Just having killed two orcs, he saw an 
opera cloak on the floor. "Oh good, I can improve my AC. What will it 
be? Protection, displacement, invisibility or magic resistance?" Of 
course, I did the pet check to make sure it was not cursed. Put it on. 
It was a poisonous cloak and the poison was deadly... I read later that 
this will kill you with a probability of 1/3, if you are not poison 
resistant. Now I knew that poisonous cloaks exist. But I had never 
encountered one so early in the game and in all previous cases when I 
put on such a cloak, it was only itchy (because I had poison 
resistance). I was not aware that they might kill you and with such a 
high probability...

Enter Icerunner, a drow. He was the most short-lived one, dying on Dlvl 
1, from a falling rock trap. Another first for me. He had 8 HP at the 
time, having lost 3 of his total in a fight before. I guess, with 11 HP, 
he would have survived. Therefore, lesson to be remembered: don't move 
around with less than 10 HP, unless you are wearing a helmet.

Icehero, the next one, human again, did a little better. He started with 
a dexterity of 18. He made it to mine town, where he managed to have his 
pet steal a magic lamp, but it was only uncursed, and he did not have a 
means to bless it. Howly was killed in the mall. The next goal was 
Sokoban. On Dlvl 10, there was a weapon store and since Icehero did not 
yet have a good weapon (still his initial quarterstaff), he looked into 
the shop -- he knew there were two long swords, via a potion of object 
detection. Got involved into a fight with a large mimic that he finally 
killed. Went to the first long sword. Wow, a long sword namede the Sword 
of Justice. The game started getting smooth. Well, not really. He picked 
it up. "You are blasted by Sword of Justice! You die... DYWYPI?" I had 
maximum hit points of 56, which should have allowed me to survive. But 
that damn fight before had brought me below 40 HP, and of course, I did 
not think. This is the first time I died from a blasting... But this is 
an experience you keep in memory. So I hope, it will not happen again.

When Iceskater was born, I sighed internally. Pathetic stats. Here they 
are, when he was at level 3.

Iceskater the Condenser    St:9 Dx:15 Co:12 In:16 Wi:10 Ch:9  Chaotic
Dlvl:3  $:381 HP:20(22) Pw:13(29) AC:6  Xp:3/110  Wt:301/575  T:1229

He was not even able to cast his level one spells, freeze sphere and 
confuse monster, with zero percent failure rate. The rate was 6 percent. 
On Dlvl 3, his pet Howly was killed by a small mimic. Also, at the time, 
I had not yet figured out the best way to use your special attack as a 
drow. Normally, I hit a monster without a weapon and then switched to 
the quarterstaff. The problem was that often the monster would wake up 
before I could finish it off and then dealt out damage. By the time my 
wand of cold was expired and I got into a desperate situation, I 
realized how to proceed better: do not use the quarterstaff at all, just 
continue hitting bare-handed (later with gloves). Since the monster 
never awakes, all but sleep resistant ones are easy, in principle.

By mine town, Iceskater was out of food. He had to rely on praying for 
some time, but once prayed too early when getting weak (probably having 
cast too many spells), so his god was displeased. But he killed a gnome 
soon and could eat to save himself from starvation. The situation became 
desperate, however, when a pixie got my quarterstaff and elven boots and 
the way back upstairs was blocked by a yeti in combination with a baby 
white dragon. Since there was a magic lamp in Izchak's shop, one of the 
precious food rations was spent to tame a large dog and get the lamp 
without paying the outrageous 1113 zorkmids for it. It was blessed!

Normally, when I get an early wish, this is for gray dragon scale mail. 
But this character was such a weakling that an improvement of his AC to 
-6 or -7 would not help a lot, because he would have to dive deeper now, 
at least down to Sokoban. If a monster hits you often, because you 
cannot kill it fast enough, then you will eventually die, even with a 
decent AC. Of course, another reason to opt for this wish is the magic 
resistance you obtain. A good compromise allowing to obtain magic 
resistance *and* a better attack would be to wish for Magicbane. I might 
have done this, but the experience with the Sword of Justice was still 
in my bones. If you want to switch frequently between your sleeping 
attack and Magicbane, you are bound to get blasted often -- as a drow, 
you are chaotic. Had I contemplated the situation in more detail, I 
might eventually still have opted for gray dragon scale mail, because I 
did have a means to attack many monsters efficiently -- but not elves. 
What contributed to my final decision was the fact that I knew there was 
a second magic lamp in another store, probably cursed, because it was 
not picked up by my dog. But I could come back later to get a second 
wish. So I wished for the Bat from Hell, a nice chaotic weapon. I did 
not miss often with it and it seemed to be the right decision, because 
now monsters went down really fast. It came in handy after I had killed 
the yeti and dragon baby, when I met a statue gargoyle. Without an 
enchanted weapon or artifact, you can only run away from these, because 
you cannot hit them. Of course, they are very slow. Moreover, they are 
not cold resistant, so you can indeed kill them with freezing spheres 
(which I realized only later).

The next good thing was a spellbook of charm monster.  I could acquire 
pets without the need to throw them food. Having a magic whistle, moving 
around with many pets was not a problem. I tamed a baby shimmering 
dragon and a baby silver dragon. Taming them is better than having to 
hit at them... In the mall, there was a mugger with a wand of fire, who 
almost made be bite the dust. I was going to enter a shop, when I was 
hit by the wand and reduced to 1 HP. But I had already a life insurance 
and immediately read my scroll of teleportation. Fortunately, it 
transported me far away. I quaffed a potion of healing and cast a spell 
of healing and then left the level as fast as possible, hoping to return 
with fire resistance.

Sokoban gave a bag of holding which was much needed -- although after 
the aforementioned experience I would have preferred an amulet of 
reflection. But Sokoban also gave me fire resistance from a red naga on 
Dlvl 8. By that time I had cold resistance by birth, sleep resistance by 
reaching level 4, poison resistance from a white unicorn on Dlvl 5, 
telepathy from a floating eye on Dlvl 6 (and a blindfold!). I would have 
to wait until Dlvl 36 to acquire further intrinsics via eating, apart 
from aggravate monster, which I obtained unintentionally when eating an 
elf (did not realize that was cannibalism for a drow). I got stealth at 
some point as a divine gift.

Having fire resistance I survived the encounter with an early soldier 
without damages beyond a burnt cloak. Got back to the mall and killed 
all muggers there (or had them killed by my baby dragons).  The plan now 
was to get enough potions from shops to make holy water (I fortunately 
had found a blessed clear potion), to go back to mine town with the help 
of the Sokoban food rations, to get the second magic lamp there and 
bless it to provide myself with gray dragon scale mail. Afterwards, I 
was hoping for my pets to take out the priest at the lawful altar (there 
was another temple with a lawful priest on Dlvl 10, so I did not risk 
protection via donations) and to convert it to appease my god, in case I 
ran out of food again. Most of the plan worked nicely. I got the lamp, 
stolen by one of my pets, blessed it, wished for gray dragon scale mail. 
My baby dragons did attack and take out the priest. I converted tha 
altar. "Air is very angry at you." and he sent in a monadic deva and a 
couatl (which I first mistook for a movanic deva). Emergency exit via a 
blessed scroll of teleportation to the upstairs. I was in no position to 
deal with a monadic deva, even with the Bat from Hell (and still missing 
sometimes) and an AC of -7.

A new plan had to be made. I considered it important to be able to 
sacrifice to mollify my god, but the altar was inaccessible for now. 
(Later I learned that the single sacrifice by which I converted the 
altar had already removed the displeasure of my god.) Going back up and 
to the main dungeon did not seem too attractive. Down to Dlvl 10, I 
could not expect many monsters nor items and below, things might become 
difficult soon. So the decision was to continue till the end of mines 
and to Ruggo the Gnome king's lair. I hoped that one of my baby dragons 
(or both) would become adult getting to kill many gnomes, gnome warriors 
and the like. In fact, Shimmy the shimmering baby dragon became a 
dragon, while Silver remained a baby silver dragon. So I went back up 
(after having picked up the luckstone and a touchstone) in the hope that 
my shimmering dragon would take out the angelic beings in mine town. (Of 
course, a shimmering dragon pet at that stage is a double-edged sword; 
if ever it went wild, my character might be dead.) My hopes shattered 
when one level below mine town we encountered a flesh golem. Shimmy 
attacked him but could not hurt him. What? I thought dragons hit as a +1 
weapon at least, and you do not need more for a flesh golem. In my 
doppelganger games before, I could always hit statue gargoyles and 
similar critters when polymorphed into a dragon. Since Shimmy survived 
down till Demogorgon's level, I could observe the following pattern 
several times: dragons like Blacky or Bluey that I had tamed as adults 
had no problems hitting vampires or vampire lords (for which you need a 
+2 or +3 weapon at least), whereas Shimmy could not hit them. I thought 
that this is a bug with the hitting ability being innate only to dragons 
created as adults but not ones that grow up from baby form. Apparently, 
however, the relevant difference is the type of dragon. Shimmering, deep 
and yellow dragons don't seem to have this ability. In the game, it 
meant I had to change plans. Essentially, I passed mine town as fast as 
possible and continued to the main dungeon, not knowing how to get at an 
altar where Iceskater could sacrifice.

As it turned out, there was an altar to Smoke on Dlvl 11. So I did not 
have to care about the one at mine town until the late game (when I 
tried to tame the monadic deva, but did not succeed, so one of my pets 
killed it). Some sacrifices gave Deep Freeze, which I never used as a 
weapon, just as a tool for fast and semipermanent engraving.

Grund's stronghold was largely uneventful. I lost a level to a wand of 
draining, but my pets killed the wand holder soon. The hell hounds I 
left to Shimmy. My intelligence and wisdom rose a bit at the end and I 
could finally cast my base spells with zero percent failure rate. By the 
way, this seems to be the first time that I did not find a wand of 
create horde in Grund's (in fact, I never found one in this game).

In a graveyard, I tamed a gug, instead of fighting it. These are 
interesting pets. They do not pick up blessed items but like cursed 
ones, which may help you identify a blessed one, but can be confusing at 
first. Also it means that if you wish to protect a stash box from being 
moved around by your pet that you have to drop a blessed and a cursed 
item in its square. What I did not know is that gugs eat rocks! This was 
a nuisance in the main dungeon, because I could not use a closet to 
safely undress and enchant a single worn piece of armor -- Guggy would 
eat the rocks around the locked door and I was exposed. Later, I made 
sure he was not on the level when I wanted to enchant my armor a bit 
more. In Gehennom, on the other hand, he was a great pet. I never needed 
my pick-axe. It was just sufficient to whistle him next to me -- he 
would often land *in* a maze wall and if not, he would still eat the 
walls around me.

As usual, I first did the neutral quest. Then part of the lawful quest. 
Having many pets, I was afraid, one of them would advance to the statue 
trap of an arch-lich; I don't know whether the lich can be woken up this 
way...

Found the portal to Ludios and decided to clean out the soldiers there, 
in spite of not having reflection yet. I was running out of food again 
(casting many spells). It worked most of the time but became a close 
call nonetheless. I stood at a knight's jump distance from the door so 
nobody could aim a wand at myself from outside. My pets did much  of the 
killing. After having killed approximately three quarters of the 
soldiers, one of them managed to zap a wand of cold at me. No big deal, 
although this showed that I was not invulnerable to wand attacks. Each 
following soldier picked up that wand even though not all of them were 
able to use it. Then a captain with a wand of lightning turned up, which 
almost ruined the game. First, I was not shock resistant, second he 
exploded one of my wands of teleportation, an unidentified ring and a 
wand of create monster. My HP went down from 101 to about 40. I cast a 
spell of charm monster which avoided further immediate HP reduction. One 
of the created monsters was a mind flayer which I teleported away with 
my second wand of teleportation. A lightning bolt whizzed by. I realized 
that the captain was now a few squares away to my southwest instead of 
standing on the neighbouring square at NE. No doubt a consequence of the 
exploded wand of teleportation. But I shot a cone of cold at him, which 
killed him; then I ran to pick up the wand of lightning and used a wand 
of extra healing on myself severalt times. I continued to finish Ludios 
-- I needed the C-rations.

But this demonstrated clearly that I badly needed reflection. I 
polymorphed a few less useful amulets, identified the result and was 
lucky: one was reflection (cursed!). I dipped it into a potion of 
restore ability to make sure it would not become strangulation again 
while being worn.

My only attack spells for a long time were, besides freeze sphere, cone 
of cold, magic missile and fireball (of which only magic missile is 
attack in Slash'em; never got beyond basic in those, but became expert 
in matter spells to which the three others belong).

I killed the lich in the Lost Tomb with a fireball, which unfortunately 
also destroyed one of his spellbooks. Later, in the Temple of Moloch, I 
made sure that my pets took care of the priests, so I got all of their 
spellbooks. Found the Spider Caves and the potions of invulnerability. 
Dragons are o.k. as pets there, if you make sure to not displace them. 
Whistle them into the spider webs and they will tear them through for 
you. Especially interesting, because I started to feel the down side of 
my drow race now: my strength did not go beyond 18. Gauntlets of power 
would have changed that but I was glad to have found swimming gauntlets 
early on, another life insurance, if I was to switch to speed boots 
later. (I had both speed and water walking boots, but wore the latter 
most of the time, casting haste self in times of need.)

The nymph level held the magic portal to One-eyed Sam's Black market. 
With seven or eight strong pets, the nymphs were not really a problem. 
Aphrodite was shot with a wand of death. One-eyed Sam had many scrolls 
of enchant weapon, of which I bought a few in exchange for gems (which I 
could easily identify thanks to my touchstone). Otherwise, I could not 
be bothered right now but decided to come back later. I also bought a 
scroll at the price of 15000, in the hope it would be genocide. It was 
charging.

I then finished Ludios and let my pets deal with the mind flayer. Kept 
1113 bullets in a box for later use. (I had already some 100 bullets 
enchanted to +4, from Grund's and shops, for later use on Vecna.)

Finishing the lawful quest and a few spicy mind flayers, I got the 
amulet of flying. Basically, I never used it, because contrary to the 
ring of levitation it does not get your carrying capacity up to 1000.

On dungeon level 29 was the downstairs to the Sunless Sea. I got the 
magic lamp, blessed it and wished for two blessed scrolls of genocide. 
Wiped out L. Somehow I overlooked a large box containing an amulet of 
magical breathing. Discovered it on my second visit much later.

Did  the Wyrm Caves and got two other dragon pets there. Unfortunately, 
there was neither a black nor a blue dragon, so I still missed two 
important intrinsics.

Several of my pets had disappeared mysteriously before the Sunless Sea, 
probably due to a level teleportation trap. Since I had to move up 
anyway to do the chaotic quest, for which I felt ready now (with lichs 
gone), wanting Vecna's hand as insurance in my proper quest, I got some 
of them back easily. Shimmy reappeared at Dlvl 18.

Did the chaotic quest, taking Guggy along, which might have been a 
mistake, because he might eat away part of my boulder fort, in 
principle. But that did not happen, as I immediately teleported close to 
Vecna, read the blessed scroll of earth and used my assault rifle. 
Needed 21 blessed +4 bullets, which is a lot. There were many spellbooks 
on the level as usual, including drain life. There also was a wand of 
cancellation. And a blessed magic lamp. Four wishes before the castle! 
This one went for a blessed rustproof +2 helm of brilliance, which 
improved my failure rates in some spells.

Then I did the quest proper (I was level 16 at the time and had an AC of 
-32). I was nervous about the Earth mage, because he is a spellcaster 
and can curse your stuff. Without a means of digging (I had wands and 
the spell), you are lost on that quest. I threw a potion of paralysis at 
him, but it was not sufficient to keep him in place long enough so I 
could kill him with the Bat. He was off after one turn. I readied my 
rifle and waited. When he appeared, I used a blessed scroll of teleport 
to try and get to the upstairs. It did not work. He had stunned me which 
I did not notice, so I landed in some unexplored place. I had one more 
blessed scroll, read it, tried to teleport to the upstairs. Sorry... 
Landed in nowhereland. This was bad. I took my cursed scroll of 
teleportation out of the bag (and put the bag back into the oilskin 
sack). Unfortunately, I did not have teleport control yet (the Mage 
still had the Storm Whistle!). So I guess the scroll tried to take me 
downward instead of upward, and I just shuddered. I had to fight off 
another attack of the Mage. He did not do a lot of damage. There was no 
easy way of proceeding to the stairs slowly, as I would have to dig my 
way through rock. So I took out my three uncursed scrolls of 
teleportation, blessed them and waited for the next attack. This time I 
made sure, I was not stunned when I read the scroll and I finally landed 
on the stairs. When the Mage arrived, I sent him bullets. Needed three 
rounds and fifteen bullets to kill him.

After having Storm Whistle, I acquired teleportitis by eating a 
leprechauns on Dlvl 32. On Dlvl 36, I discovered a black dragon via 
telepathy. Teleported there (without any scroll now!), killed it, tinned 
it, and voilà, disintegration resistance.

At Moloch's temple, I learned the spell of enchant weapon. I was expert 
at it, so I expected to be able to easily enchant my weapon, but the 
enchantment kept failing. Thought that the code for this was broken, but 
the spoilers informed me otherwise. Only every eighth enchantment does 
not fail. So the spell is largely useless, but in the long run I got 
about three points of enchantment out of it. Later, I also learned 
enchant armor, which has the same shortcomings.

Eating a gelatinous cube on Dlvl 37, I got the message "you feel wide 
awake", so apparently I had gotten sleep resistance a second time :-)... 
On Dlvl 40, there was a blue dragon, which was treated like the black 
one before, so I got shock resistance (at T=67364!).

Dlvl 41 was Medusa whom I killed in melee to get Pegasus. I tamed 
Pegasus with the spell, after three attempts. He never became useful, 
dying soon thereafter in fight.

At the Castle on Dlvl 42, I tried to find the passtune first, but since 
I had to stand at a knight's jump distance from the drawbridge, I felt 
this was too close, retreated and destroyed the drawbridge with a force 
bolt. Since I wore an oilskin cloak, the eels were straightforward. 
Finally, I decided to use the backdoor, walked around the castle and let 
most monsters fall through the trap doors. Levitated into the castle, 
did not check the weapon chamber (found later that it contained Luck 
Blade), got the wand of wishing.

In the Valley of the Dead I lost several pets including two dragons, but 
acquired some new ones in summoning storms. Moreover, I ate a tengu, 
which gave me intrinsic teleport control, so I did not have to rely on 
Storm Whistle for that anymore. I never bothered to genocide vampire 
mages, although they would definitely be worth the effort. My second 
scroll of genocide was uncursed and used on master mind flayers on the 
first level of Gehennom.

The second level of Gehennom was Juiblex's. He was the only opponent to 
ever make me sick, but with a wand of digging, he is a pushover. Next 
came Demogorgon. I refined my approach a bit so he could not even make 
me sick a single time. Usually, I read an uncursed scroll of earth on 
the upstairs, magic map the level, proceed to a place where he can see 
me and engrave Elbereth immediately afterwards. Since I never seem to 
get the timing right, he usually arrives and I get ill once. Heal 
myself, hit him, and when he teleports away, read a cursed scroll of 
teleportation or quaff a cursed potion of gain level. Then go downstairs 
and shoot him, where he cannot get at me (the stairs are blocked by a 
boulder). The refinement is not to step into his sight directly but to 
stay behind the first wall in his line of sight. Engrave Elbereth, open 
the wall with a wand of digging. He cannot touch you. Afterwards proceed 
as described.

The other demon lords can be taken in melee. But shooting them is 
sometimes more efficient. Although I did not manage to shoot Geryon in 
one round (he is the most painful after Demogrgon, because you are in 
the middle of the level when meeting him and both the upstairs and 
downstairs are far away), he did not teleport away, so I shot him where 
I met him. I did all the necessary exploration of Gehennom, this time 
without pick-axeing my way through as I had my pet gug Guggy. Killed 
Frankenstein and his monster, just to have visited this level once again 
(skipped it during my last ascension). Found the fake wizard towers and 
the vibrating square (Dlvl 58). And I was out of bullets, even though I 
had killed diamond, ruby and steel golems in melee.

To prepare for the ascension run, I teleported back to the nymph level 
and went to Sam's Black Market. Used the wand of wishing to wish for a 
blessed figurine of a giant shoggoth, which I applied in the hallway in 
front of Sam's store. The shoggoth turned out tame. Because the last 
time with this approach I had the feeling that Sam had almost killed my 
shoggoth, I decided to level him up a bit, first feeding him with Sam's 
assistants. That worked nicely, but had the consequence that Shoggy 
moved around freely instead of hiding and ate some weapons. I did not 
know that they are metallivores, but that is presumably what their acid 
is for. But he was considerate, he only engulfed Ogresmasher and spat it 
out again, much more considerate than the rust monster in another game 
at which I had thrown my Fire Brand unintentionally, which it ate (and 
did not even get tame!). Shoggy made quick work also of the cockatrices. 
When he finally arrived near Sam, I started looking around what might be 
interesting to "buy". When Sam was dead, I whistled Shoggy to an empty 
place, where he did go into hiding mode. Looted Sam's store; there were 
many scrolls of identify and teleportation, some enchant weapon, some 
enchant armor. One unknown potion = polymorph. One new spellbook: 
enlightenment. And Serpent's Tongue. There also was an uncursed scroll 
of genocide in a box, which erased mind flayers.

Since I had thought that drows cannot twoweapon, I had not considered 
that option before. (I had the +2 Sunsword in my bag of holding.) But 
after a little reading, I realized that drows are quite capable of 
twoweaponing. So I enchanted Serpent's Tongue to +6, wielded it as my 
first weapon (because I was basic at dagger) and the Bat as secondary 
weapon. This was a lot of fun, with Serpent's Tongue poisoning monsters, 
sometimes deadly. But it did not last long. I went to Ludios to pick up 
a few bullets. I had to drop my bag of holding in order not to get 
strained by the 1013 bullets. But I wanted to enchant all of them to +4 
at least, to be able to get fresh supply, if necessary. So I read a 
scroll of enchant weapon: "Your Serpent's Tongue violently glows blue 
for a while and evaporates". I had forgotten to wield the bullets! Well, 
I could still enchant them to +4, having four blessed scrolls of enchant 
weapon left. (In the meantime I had wished for a blessed magic marker.) 
I took 200 of them along and liberated Sunsword from the  bag, which I 
took as a first weapon in twoweaponing now (although it occasionally 
blasted me). The advantage was that I always had light... But now I 
needed more scrolls of enchant weapon and also of enchant amor, so I 
wished for another magic marker. I also managed to get Sunsword up by 2 
points via the enchant weapon spell. It ended up being +7, my final AC 
was -40.

I decided to prepare a bit more. There was a gypsy on Dlvl 29, with whom 
I played cards a little, which resulted (after punishment) in a wish. I 
asked for a spellbook of jumping, because jumping may be handy on 
Astral. There was another gypsy on Medusa's level, which gave me another 
wish. A spellbook of acid stream. Hopefully, the Riders are not acid 
resistant. (My magic missile spell was only at basic, being an attack 
spell. Acid stream is matter, and I was expert in that.)

Then I cleaned out the zoo in the middle wizard tower level, using a 
scroll of stinking cloud in the process. Before climbing the ladder, I 
realized  that still having two wishes in my wand, a pet Solar might be 
nice. So I teleported out and to the level below Frankenstein's. Dropped 
my pick-axe due to weight reasons. Went up into his lair, wished for the 
blessed figurine and applied it. Whoa! Tame. (If it had been hostile, I 
would have teleported it away and left the level. A Solar is dangerous. 
I would have probably survived a melee, given my AC and weapons, but 
even a melee with a crystal golem could bring me down to almost half my 
hit points. And Solars are worse.) I named him Solly.

Then on to meet the Wizard. There Guggy was a bit of a nuisance again. 
Ordinarily, I freeze all of the moat and kill all of the eels before 
digging into the Wizard's home. This time Guggy ate himself through the 
wall, once he could cross the moat. I immediately retreated and to be 
sure, the Wizard appeared at once. I used a wand of death and killed him 
with one zap.

Then things were pretty straightforward. Teleported down, did the 
invocation ritual. This time I did not teleport down into Moloch's 
Sanctum. I had no cursed potions of gain level, preferring to use up my 
last uncursed one to gain a level. (Never got beyond level 25.) Also 
with Sunsword and the Bat from Hell, the undeads should not be a major 
problem. Moreover, this was an opportunity for my Solar to level up for 
the planes. The fighting turned out almost effortless. Twoweaponing with 
two artifacts really is awesome. The minimum damage I did was 34 per hit 
(more to undeads), the average was 43 against non-undeads, if I 
calculate correctly.

The ascension run was pretty smooth. Once I had the Gehennom levels 
behind me, I switched from water walking boots to speed boots. Because 
after killing the Wizard I kept my bag of holding inside the oilskin 
sack, a pool of water created below me by some spell caster would not do 
much damage. I could swim and my stuff was protected. On the run, I kept 
dropping stuff to be able to move more items out of my bag of holding 
into my sack, where it was accessible with one move.

The Wizard turned up twice before the Plane of Earth, was killed by a 
wand of death once and by a few bullets plus the hits from Solly the 
second time. On the plane of earth, 16 bullets did not kill the Wizard, 
but he could still not do much damage, being killed by Solly in one turn 
afterwards. The portal was identified using confused reading of a scroll 
of gold detection and then my wands of digging were put to use. At the 
portal, I whistled my pets through, put on a ring of levitation and 
dropped four of my five wands of digging. They shattered, being crystal 
wands.

On Air, I moved my single wand of polymorph that I had kept out of the 
bag. I did not want to waste charges from a wand of death or a wand of 
teleportation on air elementals. So when one swallowed me, I polymorphed 
it from inside (which did not always work on the first try) and broke 
out of its stomach. First I killed the elemental afterwards, but because 
it returned to its original form albeit at half it HP, this did not 
appear very effective, so I simply moved away from the polymorphed 
elemental. There was a Titan on the level, which turned out to be the 
most remarkable event on Air. I tried to shoot him with a wand of death 
at a distance and I hit him, but titans seem to be immune. He summoned 
some nasties, but with the charm monster spell, most of them became 
friendlies immediately and attacked the unfriendlies around. At the 
portal, I dropped the wand of polymorph, whistled for my pets and went 
through. Lost one of my more interesting pets who was still eating. So 
it is really best to whistle them around until they have all gone 
through before leaving the level yourself. (Of course, you cannot lose a 
Solar this way, and he was the only pet I wanted to arrive on the Astral 
Plane at any rate.)

Fire was uneventful, but I left Yelli behind who was still eating, a 
yellow dragon and useful as cockatrice eater.

On Water I used up my remaining four scrolls of gold detection. I also 
tried whether I could not detect the portal with a wand of secret door 
detection when I was somewhat closer. That did not seem to work.

Then I arrived on Astral with the Solar, two gugs, a black dragon and an 
Olog-hai.  And an angel now. I removed the amulet of magical breathing 
and actually dropped it, in order to get below a weight of 900 before 
removing my ring of levitation. I put on life saving and readied a 
scroll of create monster and one of stinking cloud by moving them to the 
oilskin sack and the amulet of drain resistance by moving it into my 
inventory.

Famine was left, so I  went there first. Also there was a single player 
character (Chris the Necromancer) on that side and behind the temple. 
When Famine came out, I whistled Solly in front of me, who made short 
work of Famine ("Famine does not seem to hurt Solly" -- crazy, I think 
Famine was able to stun the Solar but was blinded in turn, both applied 
unicorn horns). I knew that I have 12 turns now to get far into the 
room. I stepped over Famine's corpse, teleported away everybody that was 
coming along and zapped a spell of light to see who was in the room.

The only remarkable event before entering the temple was that Chris the 
Necromancer zapped me with a wand of draining: "Good-bye level  25." 
These wands never seem to miss, which  is a bad thing. Fortunately, 
Chris was not standing before me but diagonally, so I stepped to the 
side between the walls, and exchanged my amulet of life saving for one 
of drain resistance. Then I continued fighting until Chris had moved to 
a square next to me and zapped him with a wand of death. Mine was the 
wand of draining. Things were comfortable now, because  I was surrounded 
by just a lot of harmless insects and a peaceful angel. Famine was alive 
again, but Solly stood in the room and would care for him. When I 
entered the temple, a priest turned to flee, so I knew I was in the 
right place and victory could not be taken away from me. I stepped on 
the altar and whistled in my pets (the black dragon had been killed and 
one of the gugs had been destroyed). Then Solly started to hit the 
peaceful high priest. I did not want the priest to get killed, so I 
decided to teleport him away. Useless. He resisted my magic, got angry 
and started to hit me. That's why I do not have full HP at the end. In 
order not to have to fight his high priest in Smoke's own temple, I 
offered the amulet.

The usual stuff. Choir, radiance, demigodhood.

